description:
Context : I'm rewriting the `{brochure}` package backend to rely on the `{routr}` package for the route dispatch — I've been postponing coding it myself, but now that I've found this package, this does prevent me from writing it myself 😅

Do you think it would be possible to "extract" the route dispatch so that it returns the value of the first found handler instead of needing to return `TRUE`? 

The idea would be to allow user to handle the output of the dispatch themselves. This is a very simplified example of what would need but: 

```r
first <- Route$new()
first$add_handler( 'get', '/', \(...) "hello")
second <- Route$new()
second$add_handler( 'get', '/world', \(...) "world")
routes <- RouteStack$new()
routes$add_route(
  first,
  'first'
)
routes$add_route(
  second,
  'second'
)
```

then 

```r
routes$custom_dispatct("get" "/world")
# will return
[1] "hello"
```

So this part is more of a deeper dive into what I've hacked so far to dispatch the routes in a multipage shiny app. During the dispatch, the handlers write in an external env the ui & server parts of the shiny app, but that feels like a hack. 

```r
library(routr)

# Creating the routing
routes <- RouteStack$new()

first <- Route$new()
first$add_handler(
  'get',
  '/',
  function(
    request,
    response,
    keys,
    ...
  ) {
    # This is where the "hack" happens, 
    # all handler will return TRUE to stop the routing
    # and set the UI and server in the env
    brochure_routing$ui <- fluidPage(
      h1("This is my first page"),
      plotOutput("plot")
    )
    brochure_routing$server <- function(
      input,
      output,
      session,
      extra
    ) {
      output$plot <- renderPlot({
        plot(iris)
      })
    }
    TRUE
  }
)
second <- Route$new()
second$add_handler(
  'get',
  '/profil/:name',
  function(
    request,
    response,
    keys,
    ...
  ) {
    brochure_routing$ui <- fluidPage(
      h1("This is my second page"),
      textOutput("plot")
    )
    brochure_routing$server <- function(
      input,
      output,
      session
    ) {
      output$plot <- renderPrint({
        paste("Hello", keys$name, keys$level)
      })
    }
    TRUE
  }
)
routes$add_route(
  first,
  'first'
)
routes$add_route(
  second,
  'second'
)

library(shiny)
res <- shinyApp(
  uiPattern = ".*",
  ui = function(request) {
    brochure_routing$ui
  },
  server = function(input, output, session) {
    brochure_routing$server(
      input,
      output,
      session
    )
  }
)
old_httpHandler <- res$httpHandler
brochure_routing <- new.env()
res$httpHandler <- function(req) {
  # We do the assignemnt in brochure_routing
  # here but that feels like a hack
  routes$dispatch(req)
  res <- old_httpHandler(req)
  if (!grepl("<base href", res$content)) {
    res$content <- sub(
      "<head>",
      "<head><base href=\'/'>",
      res$content,
      ignore.case = TRUE
    )
  }
  return(res)
}
res
```

What I would love to achieve is, in short: 

```r
first$add_handler(
  'get',
  '/',
  function(
    request,
    response,
    keys,
    ...
  ) {
    return(
      list(
        ui = fluidPage(
          h1("This is my first page"),
          plotOutput("plot")
        ),
        server = function(
          input,
          output,
          session,
          extra
        ) {
          output$plot <- renderPlot({
            plot(iris)
          })
        }
      )
    )
  }
)

# Then
brochure_routing <- new.env()
res$httpHandler <- function(req) {
  shiny_funs <- routes$new_dispatch_implementation(
    req
  )
  brochure_routing$ui <- shiny_funs$ui
  brochure_routing$server <- shiny_funs$server
  # ...
}
```

Does it make sense in the context of `{routr}`?

```r
library(routr)

r <- Route$new()

r$add_handler("get", "/", function(request, response, keys, ...) {
  response$status <- 200L
  response$body <- "All is good"
  return(FALSE)
})

r$remap_handlers(function(method, path, handler) {
  r$add_handler(method, paste0('/prefix', path), handler)
})
```

